<Article title="Dead Hang Standards: A Repeatable Test & Personal Benchmarks" description="Set consistent dead hang test conditions, record assistance and compare your own holds. Understand why timed hangs are not population health percentiles.">
<div class="article-header"><div class="article-breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span class="sep">/</span>Dead hang standards: make the test repeatable.</div><h1 class="article-h1">Dead hang standards: make the test repeatable.</h1><p class="article-subtitle">A useful standard starts with consistent testing. Keep the same bar, grip and assistance before comparing two hang times.</p></div>
<div class="article-body"><h2 id="test-protocol">Use the same test conditions</h2><p>A personal comparison needs a repeatable task. Choose a comfortable setup that you can leave under control. An assisted hold and an unassisted hold belong in separate records.</p><ol><li>Check the bar's fitting and load instructions. Arrange a stable step and a clear landing space.</li><li>Record the bar, grip position and assistance. Note chalk or other changes on the paper planner.</li><li>Start timing when you begin the chosen hold. Stop timing when you end it or change the agreed support.</li><li>Step down before you lose control. Stop if the movement causes pain.</li><li>Record actual seconds and completed sets. Use the same conditions at the next comparison.</li></ol><p>The <a href="/training-log/">dead hang timer and log</a> keeps assisted and unassisted best holds separate.</p><h2 id="five-levels">Choose personal benchmarks</h2><p>Beginner, intermediate and elite labels need a defined population and protocol to mean anything precise. This site does not have a validated population dataset for timed dead hangs. Earlier versions presented level bands and percentiles that the cited research did not support.</p><p>Round-number goals such as 10, 30 or 60 seconds can help organise practice. They are personal milestones rather than verified performance levels. Choose a manageable next goal instead of treating a label as a requirement.</p><h2 id="male-standards">Men's and women's comparison standards</h2><p id="female-standards">Age and sex appear in published handgrip-force reference data. A dynamometer measures squeezing force. It does not measure how long someone supports their bodyweight from a bar. Force percentiles cannot be converted directly into hanging seconds.</p><p>The <a href="/dead-hang-time-by-age/">age comparison guide</a> explains that distinction and the correction to our earlier tables.</p><h2 id="bodyweight">Record conditions that change the task</h2><p>Bodyweight, grip, bar diameter and assistance can affect the task you perform. Record a material change before interpreting a new best. This page does not apply a bodyweight multiplier because we have not validated one for these timed hangs.</p><h2 id="longevity">Keep training records separate from health scores</h2><p>A longer hold is a performance result. It is not an individual mortality estimate or a diagnostic test. Observational handgrip research does not make a particular hanging duration a longevity threshold.</p><p><a href="https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0113637">Dodds et al. (2014)</a> describe the handgrip-force tests behind their reference data.</p><h2 id="sport-standards">Follow the rules of the actual event</h2><p>A gym challenge, climbing assessment or official record attempt can use different equipment and rules. Obtain the organiser's current protocol before comparing your result with that event. A personal log entry is not an independently verified competition result.</p><h2 id="improve">Review your own change</h2><p>Compare holds performed with the same assistance. Keep a lower result in the record rather than deleting an inconvenient day. Control and consistency matter alongside a best time.</p><p>The <a href="/dead-hang-calculator/">personal hang calculator</a> shows the difference between two holds and your progress towards a target.</p><h2 id="faq">Does every adult need a 60-second hang?</h2><p>No. The sources used here do not establish 60 seconds as a universal requirement. Choose an appropriate variation and seek individual guidance if an injury or condition affects overhead exercise.</p></div>
</Article>
